  "findings": [
    {
      "description": "User-supplied URL is fetched server-side without allowlist or scheme validation in preview_url. An attacker can target internal network endpoints (SSRF), e.g. cloud metadata, internal services, or localhost. Static scan did not flag this, but the code clearly performs an unvalidated GET request to arbitrary input.",
      "category": "other",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "description": "deliver_event fetches the webhook target from the database and issues a GET request to that URL without validating scheme, host, or network scope. If hook_url can be influenced by an attacker or a compromised integration, this becomes an SSRF vector and can also trigger side effects on internal endpoints.",
      "category": "other",
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "description": "Webhook delivery is performed with GET rather than POST. Webhooks conventionally require POST, and GET delivery can cause sensitive event data to appear in logs, be cached, or be replayed by intermediaries.",
      "category": "logic-bug",
      "severity": "low"
    }
  ],
  "decision": "HOLD",
  "fix_description": "Fix the SSRF and delivery-semantic findings: (1) add a centralized URL validation helper that requires HTTPS (or an explicit allowlist) and resolves/checks the target host against RFC1918, loopback, link-local, and cloud metadata IP ranges before any request is made; (2) refactor deliver_event to send the webhook payload as a POST request with a JSON body instead of a GET query string, and reject non-webhook destinations; (3) apply the same URL/scheme/host validation in preview_url before fetching user-supplied URLs, and optionally block redirects to internal addresses. All fixes are local, minimal, and testable; the change is idempotent and can be verified with unit tests for allowlisted, private-IP, and GET-vs-POST behavior.",
